<html>
<body>

<h2>JavaScript For Loop</h2>

<p id="demo"></p>
<p id="demo1"></p>
<p id="demo2"></p>

<script>
var number = ["76.45", "091.23", "6.4573",
"2354.67", "89.9076", "3.5674", "453.9871"];

var text = "";

for (var x = 0; x < number.length; x++) {
  text += number[x] + "<br>";
}

document.getElementById("demo").innerHTML = text;

// Math.floor
document.getElementById("demo2").innerHTML =
  "Rounded number: " + Math.floor(5437.887);

// Get current date (no switch needed)
var date = new Date().getDate();

document.getElementById("demo1").innerHTML = "Today is " + date;

</script>

</body>
</html>
💡 Key Lesson
✅ new Date().getDate() → already gives correct day (1–31)
❌ Don’t use long switch for this
✅ Always match variable names (date vs today)
✅ Avoid document.write()

If you really want to use switch (for practice), I can show a correct version—but honestly, it’s not needed here.